  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 113 PM CDT Wed Aug 5 2026\n\nThe fair weather cumulus has largely been confined to western WI and \nsoutheastern MN so far this afternoon with some wispy high clouds \nalso moving through on what is otherwise a gorgeous day across the \nregion. Temperatures are comfortable in the low to mid 70s expected \nto reach the mid to upper 70s this afternoon before dropping back \ninto the 50s tonight. Tomorrow should be a bit warmer than today, \nback to the low to mid 80s as breezy southerly winds are expected \nahead of what will be our next chance for precipitation arriving on \nFriday via a weak cold front. The timing of the front has trended \nearlier within the last 24 hours of model runs, generally arriving \naround 12 hours earlier than previously forecast by most of the \nguidance as we have just reached the range where some of the CAMs \nare starting to pick up on it. The earlier timing will allow the \nstrongest showers to arrive from the northwest with a bit of \nnocturnal low level jet enhancement, however by the time it reaches \nthe MPX CWA it will be after sunrise such that they should remain \nfairly weak. Due to how weak the surface low and frontal boundary \nare, we are not expecting any sort of significant widespread rains \nor even storms from this round, with most of the area being fairly \ndry or less than a few hundredths of an inch. Those who manage to \nsee a slightly stronger shower or even a weak thunderstorm could see \na tenth of an inch or two at the most through the end of Friday.\n\nThere is still a notable warm bias within the GEFS/FV3 suite of \nguidance as we head through the weekend and into early next week, as \nthey are producing a much stronger ridge to the southwest which is \nallowing for a much greater degree of lower level warming compared \nto the much cooler EPS/ECMWF. The NBM has generally favored the EPS \nsolution with highs in the low to mid 80s through the weekend and \ninto early next week, which seems most realistic as the FV3 core \nlikely is keeping a lingering warm bias due to the hot weather we \nobserved throughout July. On top of the warm bias, there are \nconcerns due to the upstream flow being sourced from a region with \nactive wildfires resulting in upper level smoke which would help \nknock down temperatures by a degree or two. Weaker lower level \nwarming would also give us better chances for fair weather cumulus \nor even some isolated storm activity by reducing any sort of lower \nlevel cap and allowing us to tap into some instability. Given the \nupper level flow favoring west-northwest to northwesterly, some \nridge-rider style shortwave impulses are also possible, further \nadding to the daily cloud cover/isolated storm chances. All these \nthings combined, we generally favor the cooler solution compared to \nthe red hot GEFS/FV3. As we get a bit closer and into the shorter-\nCAM ranges, we should have a better idea on diurnal thunderstorm \nchances. For now, the best chances remain Friday and Saturday night, \nwith low confidence afterwards.",
